## Four-surface presentation mode

Focused fullscreen presentation now explicitly composes:
1. focused event data + media;
2. contextual timeline;
3. event-relevant relation graph;
4. simplified place map when coordinates are available.

Wide, balanced and tall fullscreen layouts reserve space for all available surfaces without changing the selected timeline orientation.

Entering fullscreen now ensures an event is focused first, so presentation mode has actual event context instead of an empty generic stage.

## Simplified presentation map

Add a read-only map controller reusing the existing lazy Leaflet/OpenStreetMap infrastructure.

- consumes the canonical event GeoJSON point;
- disables map editing, drag, wheel, touch and keyboard navigation;
- uses a simple circle marker;
- chooses a reasonable zoom from recorded location accuracy when available;
- maintains attribution;
- is created only while fullscreen presentation is active;
- is destroyed on fullscreen exit or event change.

The normal interactive location editor remains unchanged.

## Relevant graph only

When an event is focused, the global Orb surface switches to a bounded one-hop neighborhood rooted at that event.

Neighborhood traversal retains:
- the focused event;
- connected relevant entities/items/stories;
- active/timeless edges;
- otherwise-inactive relations directly changed by the focused event;
- derived activate/deactivate/update links.

Unrelated inactive topology is excluded.

## Graph physics + semantics

Improve Orb presentation:
- semantic node shapes and embedded SVG glyphs for events, stories, people/groups, places, evidence/documents, organizations, devices/accounts and generic entities;
- larger nodes and labels;
- type-dependent mass for weighted movement;
- semantic action glyph/color families for directed edges;
- stronger arrows and labels;
- continuous physics, centering and position forces for gravity-like settling and weighted drag/release.

Execution policy remains aligned with Orb itself:
- ordinary/focused graphs: worker-backed CPU force;
- very large WebGL2 graphs: GPU force;
- GPU mode is labelled `gpu-main-force` because Orb 1.0.2 documents that GPU force requires a main-thread WebGL context and cannot use its worker;
- CPU worker remains the fallback and preferred presentation path.

## Timeline range semantics

Long periods no longer lose their event label when the actual range start scrolls offscreen.

- a range terminal/connector anchors to the midpoint of the *visible* interval;
- canonical start/end text is unchanged;
- any visible part of the range retains a tracing line to its event label;
- range bars are keyboard-focusable/clickable;
- hover/focus exposes a tooltip with the event title and full range;
- event label color follows the same event/category color as its dot/range;
- semantic timeline/tag/relation/evidence icons are enlarged.

## Verification

Tests cover:
- midpoint visible-range anchoring;
- read-only presentation-map behavior and zoom policy;
- simultaneous fullscreen timeline/graph/map surfaces;
- event-relevant graph scoping;
- semantic Orb icon/shape/physics configuration;
- worker CPU vs explicit GPU main-thread mode labels;
- range tooltip/focus behavior;
